WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Hey, welcome to the D.P. Pod, the due positive podcast, episode two.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.400
I'm Dan Perra, joined by Patrick Novak, it's July 5th, 2026.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.520
It's exactly what it sounds like, the good news, the honest numbers, and something to do about it.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.240
You know what stuck with me this week?

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.540
How often the simplest tweaks or vibes when the fancy hardware doesn't?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.860
The diesel one, right?

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:21.560
Exactly.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.780
Mixing water into diesel can cut engine emissions by 67% with no efficiency loss.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.600
A change any diesel user can test.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.000
That feels like the kind of thing we actually get to try ourselves

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.940
instead of waiting for a policy memo.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.720
Time for the positive papers.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.800
Start with the anti-ferro magnets.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.320
A German-Japanese research team involving the University of Augsburg has made a significant breakthrough

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.220
in the use of anti-ferro magnets.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.260
For the first time, the team has succeeded in writing magnetic information

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.540
using only ultra-short laser pulses without the need for electric currents or magnetic fields.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.140
The method eliminates the need for electric currents or external magnetic fields

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.400
that normally consume power and generate heat.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.160
Anti-ferro magnets store data in opposing spin alignments that produce no net magnetic field,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.500
making them stable and fast to switch.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.300
Early demonstrations show the optical route works at room temperature on relevant materials.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.160
The approach remains at the proof-of-concept stage with no commercial devices yet built

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.780
and scaling the laser control to dense arrays is still unproven.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.320
The breakthrough matters because data centers and memory systems currently rely on

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.120
energy-intensive methods that produce excess heat.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.640
Optical control could change how information is handled at the smallest scales without those overheads.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.920
The mechanism is the part that gets me.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:46.140
Anti-ferro magnets store information in opposing spin alignments that cancel out any net field,

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.300
so they're stable and switch fast.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:53.900
Optical pulses let them flip without the heat penalty that normally comes from running current

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.060
through memory cells.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.640
OK, but data centers already run on massive cooling loops.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.180
If this stays proof-of-concept, how many years until a rack actually uses it?

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.080
Fair question.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.120
The briefing is clear, it's still at the room temperature demonstration stage,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.880
and scaling the laser arrays is unproven.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.280
I won't over claim.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.540
So we file it under promising but watch the heat numbers.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.160
Next story is the optical centrifuge for superfluids.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:26.300
Physicists have developed a new optical centrifuge that can precisely spin molecules inside a superfluid

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.280
for the first time.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.120
The advance could help unravel some of the biggest mysteries of quantum liquids

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.980
and reveal how superfluidity breaks down at the atomic scale.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.300
The tool lets researchers watch how quantum liquids lose their frictionless property

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.600
at the atomic scale.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.160
By isolating rotation as the variable, the centrifuge reveals breakdown mechanisms

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.980
that bulk measurements had hidden.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.940
The work is still limited to laboratory scale samples and controlled conditions.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:01.300
Success here would improve models used in quantum sensors and low-loss energy transport systems.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:07.300
The development stands out because superfluids exhibit zero viscosity under certain conditions,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.400
yet the precise triggers for losing that state have remained difficult to isolate until now.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.640
Understanding those triggers at the molecular level could inform future designs in

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.140
precision measurement devices.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.860
What's the practical angle there?

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.520
If they can isolate the breakdown triggers at the molecular level,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.840
the models improve for quantum sensors and low-loss energy transport.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.580
Still lab scale only, but it's the kind of measurement that usually precedes better devices.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.100
So two stories about co-entrolling quantum behavior with light.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.960
Both elegant, both early.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.420
Then the diesel result.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.180
A Nigerian research team reviewed studies from multiple groups showing

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:52.960
that a stable water diesel emulsion cuts particulate and nox emissions in the 60-67% range,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.960
while power stays flat or edges up slightly.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.560
The mechanism is combustion chemistry changing because of the emulsion.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.300
No new injectors required.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:06.540
Right, the catch is keeping the emulsion stable across real fuel batches and temperatures.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.780
But the review says results held across varied engine types when the water fraction

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.160
stayed in the tested window.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.240
I like that the builders here are just aggregating existing tests instead of chasing a new patent.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.000
Sometimes the wind is proving the obvious works.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.500
Time to think positive.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.860
James Clear's work on atomic habits shows that identity shifts follow repeated small actions

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.380
rather than the other way around.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.240
When a person performs one concrete step such as running a quick test on a fuel mixture,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.700
they begin to see themselves as someone who solves problems instead of waiting for solutions.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.280
That loop shows up in the lab teams too.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.440
The laser pulse group kept refining one variable at a time

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.180
until optical writing worked at room temperature.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.040
Same pattern with the centrifuge team isolating rotation.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.580
So the mental rep is to name one physical action you can finish in under 10 minutes

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.540
and do it before you check another feed.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.180
That brings us to the lever.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.500
If you run a diesel engine, it makes a small measured volume of water into the fuel

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.500
following published emulsion protocols.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.640
Studies reviewed in the source show roughly 60 to 67% lower particulate

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.320
and nox emissions while power output stays the same or improves slightly.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.680
Cost is just distilled water and a few minutes per batch.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:19.920
No new hardware.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.980
If one in 10 diesel operators tried it, transport emissions could drop several percent in a year.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:26.280
That's the scale claim.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.400
I'm not sold.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.040
It's automatic at fleet level.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.780
Real world fuel quality and ambient conditions can break the emulsion.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.860
So the 60 to 67% number might shrink once operators start measuring.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:39.680
That's fair.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.240
The briefing hedges it as rough because results vary with engine type.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.460
Still, the barrier is low enough that a single tank test costs almost nothing.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:48.080
Okay.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.100
The payback math I'll give you.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.280
Operators already tracking fuel logs can add it without new routines.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.880
I'm doing this one this week on the next fill up.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.180
I'll report back with whatever the gauges say.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.160
Now the do positive dispatch.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.340
No dispatches in the bag today.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:07.640
Check back tomorrow for the first listener reports on last week's heat pump filter swap.

